+# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 # Design analysis for the D10 variant
-# ==================================
-# a) concretization_and_trace:
-#    - The scene is still two large colored regions on black background, with black
-#      dents and one-cell noise. The visible change is only in the output patches.
-#    - Original behavior at a dent:
-#      1. Clean stray fg cells / boundary protrusions.
-#      2. Detect that a black cell is surrounded mostly by one host color.
-#      3. Stamp a fixed 3x3 opposite-color square and recolor the center teal.
-#    - Variant behavior at a dent:
-#      1. Keep the same cleanup and host-color inference.
-#      2. Restore the dent cell conceptually to its host color, then measure the
-#         Chebyshev distance from the center to the nearest non-host cell or grid
-#         edge in the cleaned host region.
-#      3. Stamp an opposite-color square of radius equal to that distance:
-#         distance 1 -> 3x3, distance 2 -> 5x5, distance 3 -> 7x7.
-#      4. Recolor the original dent center teal.
-#    - Concrete example:
-#      A dent one step from a region edge has a mixed 3x3 neighborhood, so the
-#      first failing ring is radius 1 and the output remains a 3x3 patch.
-#      A deeper dent with a full host-colored 3x3 but an interrupted 5x5 has
-#      radius 2, so the output grows to 5x5. This is the exact D10 difference.
-# b) complexity_calibration:
-#    - Patch size is not continuous in an arbitrary way: it is quantized by square
-#      rings, which keeps the rule learnable.
-#    - The generator purposely produces multiple radius levels per example, with at
-#      least two distinct sizes and at least two dents of radius >= 2, so the new
-#      behavior is visible from a small number of ARC samples.
-#    - Dents are spaced far enough apart that their variable patches do not merge,
-#      avoiding ambiguity about where one scaled patch ends and another begins.
-# c) implicit_assumption_review:
-#    - Original solver assumed every dent uses radius 1 because it always stamped
-#      `r-1:r+2, c-1:c+2`. That is no longer valid.
-#    - Original scan-order overwriting was harmless because equal-size patches had
-#      limited overlap. In the variant, larger squares could overwrite each other,
-#      so the generator guarantees separation; the solver still behaves sensibly on
-#      arbitrary inputs by stamping in row-major dent order and restoring teal last.
-#    - Host inference from a cleaned 3x3 remains valid; only the stamping radius
-#      changes.
-# d) placement_legality:
-#    - Candidate dents are generated only on clean host cells whose 3x3 majority
-#      still identifies the host after blackening the center.
-#    - Selected dents must satisfy a gap based on both radii, so output squares stay
-#      disjoint and the input dents do not corrupt each other's local majority.
-#    - The generator only selects dents with radii in {1,2,3}; the solver supports
-#      any larger radius, but keeping 3 visible tiers is cleaner and sufficient.
-# e) visual_observability:
-#    - Humans can compare nearby 3x3, 5x5, and 7x7 opposite-color blocks, each with
-#      the same teal center marker, and infer that deeper dents cause larger blocks.
-#    - No color-id-based ordering is used to define behavior. Colors only play the
-#      original host/other-region roles that are visible in the grids.
-# f) visual_cleanliness:
-#    - Two-cell dent pairs are disabled here because adjacent variable-size squares
-#      create clutter and obscure the new scaling cue.
-#    - Noise is kept, but dent count is slightly reduced relative to the seed so the
-#      patch-size variation remains easy to read.
-# g) color_change_inference:
-#    - Color mapping stays identical to the seed task: each dent becomes a patch in
-#      the opposite foreground color, and the center is teal. The only new degree of
-#      freedom is patch size, which is tied to visible spatial depth.
-# h) generator_validation_target:
-#    - Oracle checking still uses `main(input) == output`.
-#    - Additional post-hoc validation should confirm that generated samples contain
-#      multiple radii, rather than accidentally degenerating to all 3x3 outputs.
-# i) solver_assumptions_after_change:
-#    - Every true dent is still an original black cell whose cleaned 3x3 has a
-#      unique majority host color with count >= 5.
-#    - `generate_input()` guarantees these assumptions by choosing dent centers from
-#      the clean regions, blackening only those cells, and validating the final grid
-#      with the solver.
+#
+# concretization:
+#   - The two large foreground regions and the black background remain unchanged.
+#   - The input still contains isolated wrong-color specks and cross-boundary
+#     contamination, so the cleanup stage remains necessary.
+#   - The changed object is the opposite-color patch produced around each dent.
+#     In the original task every valid dent produced a fixed 3x3 square.
+#     Here, each dent produces the largest odd square supported by the host
+#     region around that dent: if the dent has one full same-color ring around
+#     it, output 3x3; two rings, output 5x5; three rings, output 7x7.
+#   - Concretely, after cleanup, a dent at (r,c) hosted by color A has radius d
+#     iff every cell in the square [r-d:r+d, c-d:c+d] except the center is A,
+#     and radius d+1 fails. The output stamps a (2d+1)x(2d+1) square in color B
+#     and then recolors the center teal.
+#
+# behavior trace:
+#   - Original logic at a dent: detect host from cleaned 3x3 majority -> always
+#     stamp radius 1.
+#   - New logic at a dent: detect host from cleaned 3x3 majority -> expand the
+#     checked square while all non-center cells stay host-colored -> stamp the
+#     resulting radius d.
+#   - Example: a dent with host rings of widths 1, 2, 3 behaves as 3x3, 5x5,
+#     7x7 respectively. This is the only semantic change in the transform.
+#
+# complexity calibration:
+#   - If the generator allowed only radius-1 dents, the variant would visually
+#     collapse back to the original task. To keep D10 observable, each sample is
+#     forced to include several dents with radius > 1 and at least one 7x7 case.
+#   - To avoid introducing unrelated ambiguity, the generator only uses three
+#     repeated tiers (radii 1/2/3), not a wide unconstrained range.
+#
+# hidden assumptions reviewed:
+#   - main() originally assumed every detected dent should stamp exactly one
+#     clipped 3x3 square. That assumption fails under D10, so the solver now
+#     computes a per-dent radius before stamping.
+#   - The old dent detector accepted boundary dents with only 5 host cells in
+#     the 3x3 neighborhood. Under D10 that would create ambiguous "distance"
+#     values. The new solver still uses the 3x3 majority to infer the host, but
+#     only accepts dents whose host support expands to at least one full ring.
+#   - Because patch sizes now vary, overlapping large patches would hide the
+#     scaling cue. The generator therefore spaces dents so their stamped squares
+#     do not overlap.
+#
+# placement legality:
+#   - Candidate dent cells are chosen from the clean regions before blackening.
+#     Their exact support radius is measured on the clean grid.
+#   - Dents are selected with Chebyshev spacing greater than the sum of their
+#     radii, so opposite-color squares stay disjoint and fully inside the host
+#     region that justified the radius.
+#   - Noise placement skips protected neighborhoods around dents so cleanup
+#     remains stable and the solver's inferred radii match the intended output.
+#
